# BizMate AI AI Architecture
Version: 1.0
Status: Approved
Last Updated: 2026-07-26

---

# 1. Purpose

This document defines the Artificial Intelligence architecture for BizMate AI.

It specifies how AI understands user requests, processes business information, validates decisions, interacts with users, and assists with daily business operations.

The AI is an assistant—not an autonomous decision-maker.

All financial operations require explicit user confirmation before they are committed.

---

# 2. AI Design Principles

The AI layer follows these principles:

- Human-in-the-loop
- Explainable
- Predictable
- Secure
- Multilingual
- Context-aware
- Business-focused
- Privacy-first
- Extensible

---

# 3. AI Responsibilities

The AI is responsible for:

- Understanding voice commands
- Understanding typed commands
- Detecting user intent
- Extracting business entities
- Identifying duplicate records
- Suggesting corrections
- Answering business questions
- Generating summaries
- Providing daily business insights
- Assisting with navigation
- Helping users complete tasks faster

The AI is NOT responsible for:

- Automatically recording financial transactions
- Inventing customers
- Inventing products
- Guessing prices
- Guessing balances
- Deleting business data without confirmation

---

# 4. AI Processing Pipeline

Every AI request follows the same pipeline.

```
User Input
      │
      ▼
Language Detection
      │
      ▼
Speech-to-Text (if voice)
      │
      ▼
Intent Detection
      │
      ▼
Entity Extraction
      │
      ▼
Duplicate Detection
      │
      ▼
Rules Engine Validation
      │
      ▼
Confidence Scoring
      │
      ▼
Confirmation Card
      │
      ▼
Database Write
      │
      ▼
Timeline Event
      │
      ▼
Audit Log
      │
      ▼
AI Memory Update
```

Every financial write passes through this pipeline.

---

# 5. AI Modules

The AI subsystem consists of:

- Language Detection
- Speech Recognition
- Intent Engine
- Entity Extraction Engine
- Duplicate Detection Engine
- Rules Engine
- Confidence Engine
- Confirmation Engine
- AI Memory
- Recommendation Engine
- Analytics Engine

Each module performs one responsibility only.

---

# 6. Language Support

Version 1 supports:

- English
- Nigerian Pidgin
- Yoruba
- Hausa
- Igbo

Future versions may support additional languages.

Users may switch languages at any time.

Mixed-language conversations should be supported where practical.

---

# 7. Intent Recognition

The AI must classify user requests into business intents.

Examples:

Create Customer

Record Sale

Receive Payment

Record Expense

Search Customer

Search Product

Generate Report

Business Question

Settings

General Conversation

If the intent cannot be determined confidently, clarification is required.

---

# 8. Entity Extraction

The AI extracts business entities such as:

Customer Name

Product Name

Amount

Payment Method

Sale Type

Expense Category

Date

Reference Number

Notes

Every extracted entity receives a confidence score.

---

# 9. Confidence Scoring

Each recognised entity is assigned a confidence percentage.

Example:

Customer

98%

Product

96%

Payment Type

100%

Amount

99%

If confidence falls below the configured threshold, the AI asks for clarification before proceeding.

---

# 10. Duplicate Detection

Duplicate detection applies before creating:

Customers

Products

Categories

Suppliers (future)

Branches (future)

Detection methods include:

- Exact Match
- Case-Insensitive Match
- Phonetic Match
- Fuzzy Match
- Semantic Match
- Historical Confirmation

Potential duplicates are presented to the user before creation.

---

# 11. Confirmation Workflow

The AI never performs financial writes immediately.

Instead, it displays a confirmation summary.

Example:

Customer:
Ade Stores

Product:
HP ProDesk

Amount:
₦320,000

Payment:
Credit

Outstanding:
₦320,000

Buttons:

Confirm

Edit

Cancel

Only "Confirm" commits the transaction.

---

# 12. Rules Engine Integration

Every confirmed request is validated by the Rules Engine.

Checks include:

Customer exists

Product exists

Price valid

Payment valid

Business ownership

User permissions

Duplicate prevention

If validation fails, the transaction is rejected with an explanation.

---

# 13. AI Memory

AI Memory stores only confirmed business knowledge.

Examples:

Customer aliases

Frequently purchased products

Preferred payment methods

Frequently used descriptions

Business terminology

Frequently asked questions

The AI never stores assumptions or guesses.
---

# 14. AI Provider Abstraction Layer

BizMate AI must remain independent of any single AI provider.

The AI layer communicates through a provider abstraction interface.

Supported providers include:

- OpenAI
- Google Gemini
- Anthropic Claude
- Lovable AI Gateway
- Future local AI models

Business logic must never directly depend on a provider-specific SDK.

Changing providers should require only configuration changes.

---

